From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 03:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/ac3dec: do not start as if the delay samples were
 downmixed

ac3_decode_init() set downmixed to 1, which says that the delay samples
hold the output channels of a downmix. A decoder that does not downmix
never clears it in its long blocks, so the first block in which the
channels use different transforms (block switching in some of them only)
calls ac3_upmix_delay() on delay samples that were never downmixed: for
stereo and dual mono it copies the delay of the left channel over that
of the right one; for 2F1R and 2F2R it zeroes the surround channels'; for
3F, 3F1R and 3F2R it moves the centre's into the right one and zeroes the
centre's and the surrounds'. The first half of that block of those
channels is then made with the overlap of another channel or with none.

A flush leaves downmixed at 0 (ac3_decode_flush() clears it with the rest
of the state), and a decoder that downmixes sets it before it first uses
the delay samples: there is no state in which 1 is needed before the
first block. Start with 0, as the zeroed context has it.

The decoded output of a stream that is downmixed does not change; a
stream that is not changes in one block of one frame, the first one that
switches blocks in some channels only, and is then what the same decoder
gives after a flush.

FATE: two of the samples have such a block, and their references were
made with the defect:
- ac3-2.0 and ac3-fixed-2.0 (monsters_inc_2.0_192_small.ac3): frame 122,
  block 4, the right channel (up to 187 in 16 bits);
- ac3-4.0 (millers_crossing_4.0.ac3, 3F1R): frame 38, block 5, the right
  and the centre channels (up to 1693 and 193).
Their new references, monsters_inc_2.0_192_small_v3.pcm and
millers_crossing_4.0_v3.pcm, are the output of the fixed float decoder,
and differ from the _v2 ones by more than 1 only in those blocks; the
fixed-point decoder matches the 2.0 one within oneoff, as it did the _v2
one. Every other test that uses the AC-3 or E-AC-3 decoders or encoders
(the downmix tests, E-AC-3, the encoders, the demuxers and remuxers that
probe AC-3) gives what it gave.
  sha256 d28f76d35d08e2c278e00ec5127b0122375ac6807a8bd2151f182a5a41bbf72b  monsters_inc_2.0_192_small_v3.pcm
  sha256 4b3816f57200415aa4314d6079a0efcfd8e46392382a30ea52843ba18d8f50ed  millers_crossing_4.0_v3.pcm
---
 libavcodec/ac3dec.c | 1 -
 tests/fate/ac3.mak  | 6 +++---
 2 files changed, 3 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libavcodec/ac3dec.c b/libavcodec/ac3dec.c
--- a/libavcodec/ac3dec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/ac3dec.c
@@ -130,7 +130,6 @@
         avctx->sample_fmt = AV_SAMPLE_FMT_FLTP;
 
     ac3_downmix(avctx);
-    s->downmixed = 1;
 
     for (i = 0; i < AC3_MAX_CHANNELS; i++) {
         s->xcfptr[i] = s->transform_coeffs[i];
diff --git a/tests/fate/ac3.mak b/tests/fate/ac3.mak
--- a/tests/fate/ac3.mak
+++ b/tests/fate/ac3.mak
@@ -1,10 +1,10 @@
 FATE_AC3 += fate-ac3-2.0
 fate-ac3-2.0: CMD = pcm -i $(TARGET_SAMPLES)/ac3/monsters_inc_2.0_192_small.ac3
-fate-ac3-2.0: REF = $(SAMPLES)/ac3/monsters_inc_2.0_192_small_v2.pcm
+fate-ac3-2.0: REF = $(SAMPLES)/ac3/monsters_inc_2.0_192_small_v3.pcm
 
 FATE_AC3 += fate-ac3-4.0
 fate-ac3-4.0: CMD = pcm -i $(TARGET_SAMPLES)/ac3/millers_crossing_4.0.ac3
-fate-ac3-4.0: REF = $(SAMPLES)/ac3/millers_crossing_4.0_v2.pcm
+fate-ac3-4.0: REF = $(SAMPLES)/ac3/millers_crossing_4.0_v3.pcm
 
 #downmix 4.0 -> front channel
 FATE_AC3 += fate-ac3-4.0-downmix-mono
@@ -29,7 +29,7 @@
 
 FATE_AC3 += fate-ac3-fixed-2.0
 fate-ac3-fixed-2.0: CMD = pcm -c ac3_fixed -i $(TARGET_SAMPLES)/ac3/monsters_inc_2.0_192_small.ac3
-fate-ac3-fixed-2.0: REF = $(SAMPLES)/ac3/monsters_inc_2.0_192_small_v2.pcm
+fate-ac3-fixed-2.0: REF = $(SAMPLES)/ac3/monsters_inc_2.0_192_small_v3.pcm
 
 FATE_AC3 += fate-ac3-fixed-4.0-downmix-mono
 fate-ac3-fixed-4.0-downmix-mono: CMD = pcm -c ac3_fixed -downmix mono -i $(TARGET_SAMPLES)/ac3/millers_crossing_4.0.ac3
